FreedomMoniesTalk
³3newauthor³31³3minor³31³2host³2222.248.18.10³2username³2³2revision³29³2id³2111³2tscreate³21095455851³2keepts³21100667625³1ip³2205.231.31.86³2ts³21100667625³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3= Freedom Moneys =

[Note: replace monies in title too. the subj is enough without 'monies'.]

Freedom moneys are moneys that advance or at least don't impair the concept of freedom. In general the less the govt has to do with controlling a money the freer that money is, and the more of a freedom money it is.

The finest freedom money is of course gold, followed very closely by silver. But, new freedom moneys or rather currencies are appearing:

Net engendered currencies or eMetals like eGold, etc.

Generally, many already understand some of the advantages of this. Ultimately, that needs to be addressed, but for now, I want to tighten the commerce by tracking usages and costs. As in--

#Cost to keep gold there.
#Convenience to get value in there.
#Convenience to get gold in there.
#Cost to transfer value out.
#Cost to transfer gold out.
#Cost to cash out.
#Convenience to cash out.
#Anonymity.
#Rated trust of company.

Maybe a table of these and the companies offering will do for a bit. There are already a number of companies competing in this market, but all is still developing. The starting point has unfortunately been business models that have developed or been distorted by many years of government interference. It will take some time to recover.

My opinion here concerns particularly the privacy or anonymity involved in the services and the fee schedules. The forerunner in this is Digital Money Trust (DMT) of [http://orlingrabbe.com Orlin Grabbe]. DMT offers total anonymity and DMT charges 1/10 of 1% for internal transfers (where 1% is seen as 'normal'). I would expect all computer-driven transfer fees to approach zero.

Note that often it 'costs' 1% p.a. to just hold the gold. So after forty years, you retire with 60% of what you saved. Ten pounds of gold is a 'small' bar or 120 one-ounce coins and is currently worth about $50,000. Perhaps a safe is in order.

- - -

<b>Gold, Silver, Platinum, Palladium, and Rhodium Bullions</b>

They all all working their way into the commodity money mix. As of 02 Oct '04:

||Metal||$/oz||
||Gold||$420||
||Silver||$6.92||
||Platinum||$872||
||Palladium||$236||
||Rhodium||$1300||

E-gold Ltd, a Nevis corporation, 100% backed at all times by gold bullion in allocated storage... claims registered trademark to e-gold, e-metal, e-silver, e-platinum, and e-palladium, afaik. Interesting IP bandwagen.

Then, there's Digital Money Trust (DMT) of [http://orlingrabbe.com Orlin Grabbe] for anonymous security.

- - -

From kbarrett's post from TCF:

E-gold is a US based entity .... but you can trade e-gold for other anonymous metal based currencies, like pecunix.

[https://www.1mdc.com/ If you move your gold into 1mdc, it will become anonymous.]

You can then trade it out from there using [http://open2exchange.com/buy.sell.. Open2Exchange].

If you need help funding, these folks can help: [http://www.goldnow.st/Page.aspx GoldNow]

And finally, I would suggest checking any service out [http://www.gdcaonline.org/ here first.] Only use Silver or better rated members.

